      "Record the anesthesia unit support close reason, the approver, and the surviving evidence reference in the same case note. Use the same definition across the queue and the review so the counts agree. Keep the original request so a later correction does not erase the reason for the work. Compare the destination field, not only the activity log. Every anesthesia unit support handoff names the coding lead and the field in the claim file that will confirm the change. At Outsourced Billing Services, the anesthesia unit support queue marks a held claim with the reason it stopped and the person who can release it.",
      "A case that moves between owners should keep both owner periods visible. Record the approximate value, the approver, and the final check time. Do not let a deadline expand a role beyond its written limit. Keep the prior value so the change can be explained. In the anesthesia unit support queue, an item without a next action is treated as an exception. At Outsourced Billing Services, the anesthesia unit support queue marks a held claim with the reason it stopped and the person who can release it.",
      "## Limitations",
      "Review anesthesia unit support with a sample drawn from the queue export before the meeting, so the discussion cannot drift to memorable cases. Include a routine case, a reopened case, and a cross-owner handoff. Name the owner for every open item, including items waiting on someone else. The record should show the source, the decision, and the surviving value in one place. For anesthesia unit support, compare the request record with the destination value rather than relying on a status label. Outsourced Billing Services keeps the anesthesia unit support source, reviewer, and decision date together in the claim file so a later check does not depend on memory.",
      "## Review and next action",
